In TypeORM, you can use the `json` or `jsonb` type to store JSON data in a PostgreSQL database. Here's an example of how you can define a field of type `json` in your entity:

```typescript
import { Entity, PrimaryGeneratedColumn, Column } from "typeorm";

@Entity()
export class YourEntity {
    @PrimaryGeneratedColumn()
    id: number;

    @Column({ type: "json" })
    yourJsonField: object;
}
```

In this example, `yourJsonField` is a field that can store JSON data.

The `json` type stores JSON data as a single JSON value, while the `jsonb` type stores JSON data as a JSONB value, which supports indexing and is more efficient for querying. However, the `jsonb` type does not support JSON functions like `->>` and `->`.

You can choose the type that best fits your use case. For more information, you can refer to the TypeORM documentation:

- [TypeORM JSON type](https://typeorm.io/#/guide/typing-json

Here's a sample Spark job that calculates the hit rate for onsite attributed data:

```java
import org.apache.spark.api.java.JavaPairRDD;
import org.apache.spark.api.java.JavaRDD;
import org.apache.spark.api.java.function.Function;
import org.apache.spark.api.java.function.PairFunction;
import scala.Tuple2;

import java.sql.Timestamp;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class HitRateCalculator {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Create Spark session
        java.util.Properties props = new java.util.Properties();
        props.setProperty("hadoop.home.dir", "/usr/local/hadoop");
        JavaSparkContext sc = new JavaSparkContext(props);
        JavaSparkContext.hadoopConfiguration().set("hadoop.job.ugi", "hdfs");
        JavaSparkContext.hadoopConfiguration().set("hadoop.job.group", "supergroup");

        // Load onsite attributed data
        JavaRDD<String> onsiteAttributedData = sc.textFile("path/to/onsite_attributed_data.csv");
        JavaRDD<String[]> onsiteAttributedDataRows = onsiteAttributedData.map(new Function<String, String[]>() {
            @Override
            public String[] call(String line) throws Exception {
                return line.split(",");
            }
        });
        JavaPairRDD<String, Timestamp> onsiteAttributedDataPairs = onsiteAttributedDataRows.mapToPair(new PairFunction<String[], String, Timestamp>() {
            @Override
            public Tuple2<String, Timestamp> call(String[] line) throws Exception {
                return new Tuple2<>(line[0], new Timestamp(Long.parseLong(line[1]) * 1000));
            }
        });

        // Load top frequent queries
        JavaRDD<String> topFrequentQueries = sc.textFile("path/to/top_frequent_queries.txt");
        Set<String> topQueries = new HashSet<>();
        for (String line : topFrequentQueries.collect()) {
            topQueries.add(line);
        }

        // Initialize cache and hit count
        Map<String, Timestamp> cache = new HashMap<>();
        long hitCount = 0;
        long totalCount = 0;

        // Process onsite attributed data
        onsiteAttributedDataPairs.foreach(new Function<Tuple2<String, Timestamp>, Void>() {
            @Override
            public Void call(Tuple2<String, Timestamp> pair) throws Exception {
                String query = pair._1();
                Timestamp timestamp = pair._2();

                // Check if query is in cache and if it's a cache hit
                if (cache.containsKey(query) && timestamp.after(cache.get(query).getTime() + 43200000)) {
                    hitCount++;
                } else {
                    // Check if query is in top frequent queries
                    if (topQueries.contains(query)) {
                        // If it's a cache miss, add query to cache
                        cache.put(query, timestamp);
                    }
                }
                totalCount++;
                return null;
            }
        });

        // Calculate hit rate
        double hitRate = (double) hitCount / totalCount;
        System.out.println("Hit rate: " + hitRate);

        // Stop Spark context
        sc.stop();
    }
}
```

This job reads the onsite attributed data from a CSV file and the top frequent queries from a text file. It processes the onsite attributed data, checks if each query is in the cache and if it's a cache hit, increments the hit count. If the query is not in the cache and it's in the top frequent queries, it adds the query to the cache. After processing all the data, it calculates the hit rate and prints it.

